DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       sseetteeuuiidd()  sets the effective user ID of the calling process.  Unprivi‐
       leged processes may only set the effective user ID to the real user ID,
       the effective user ID or the saved set‐user‐ID.

       Precisely the same holds for sseetteeggiidd() with "group" instead of "user".

RREETTUURRNN VVAALLUUEE
       On success, zero is returned.  On error, -1 is returned, and  _e_r_r_n_o  is
       set to indicate the error.

       _N_o_t_e:  there are cases where sseetteeuuiidd() can fail even when the caller is
       UID 0; it is a grave security error to omit checking for a failure  re‐
       turn from sseetteeuuiidd().

EERRRROORRSS
       EEIINNVVAALL The target user or group ID is not valid in this user namespace.

       EEPPEERRMM  In  the case of sseetteeuuiidd(): the calling process is not privileged
              (does not have the CCAAPP__SSEETTUUIIDD capability in its user  namespace)
              and _e_u_i_d does not match the current real user ID, current effec‐
              tive user ID, or current saved set‐user‐ID.

              In  the case of sseetteeggiidd(): the calling process is not privileged
              (does not have the CCAAPP__SSEETTGGIIDD capability in its user  namespace)
              and  _e_g_i_d  does not match the current real group ID, current ef‐
              fective group ID, or current saved set‐group‐ID.

VVEERRSSIIOONNSS
       Setting the effective user (group) ID to the saved  set‐user‐ID  (saved
       set‐group‐ID) is possible since Linux 1.1.37 (1.1.38).  On an arbitrary
       system one should check __PPOOSSIIXX__SSAAVVEEDD__IIDDSS.

       Under  glibc 2.0, sseetteeuuiidd((_e_u_i_d)) is equivalent to sseettrreeuuiidd((--11,, _e_u_i_d)) and
       hence may change the saved set‐user‐ID.  Under glibc 2.1 and later,  it
       is  equivalent to sseettrreessuuiidd((--11,, _e_u_i_d,, --11)) and hence does not change the
       saved set‐user‐ID.  Analogous remarks hold for sseetteeggiidd(), with the dif‐
       ference that the change in implementation from  sseettrreeggiidd((--11,,  _e_g_i_d))  to
       sseettrreessggiidd((--11,,  _e_g_i_d,, --11)) occurred in glibc 2.2 or 2.3 (depending on the
       hardware architecture).

       According to POSIX.1, sseetteeuuiidd() (sseetteeggiidd()) need not permit _e_u_i_d (_e_g_i_d)
       to be the same value as the current effective user (group) ID, and some
       implementations do not permit this.

